#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5d92700-fe95-5d2c-8b25-5b6e3af31411" :knowledge-point/title "Applying a Percentage Decrease: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08ba13fe-1d0b-507b-85bb-128d69277956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$74.25$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508400} {:answer/id #uuid "0fc34084-dac6-528b-9bab-3c95b340ec89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$71.50$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508413} {:answer/id #uuid "18ab41f3-9e7c-549d-8e33-45a4d949c8b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$73.00$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508426} {:answer/id #uuid "5cfcbd7c-3cad-5dc2-9e79-693fbe4c4b98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$70.00$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508528} {:answer/id #uuid "a18f0694-e14d-5621-a0cd-e736598ab64d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$72.25$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a18f0694-e14d-5621-a0cd-e736598ab64d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$72.25$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508633} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5312bb0-f9b0-5ad2-9bbb-517845ea0b67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7719fa6-fde6-59d2-a1a3-a59122c8717b" :question/math-academy-id "q-326544" :question/problem "An office chair is marked down by $15\\%$ during a promotion. What is the sale price of the chair if its original price is $\\$85?$" :question/worked-solution "To find an amount that's $15\\%$ *less than* $\\$85,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%-15\\%=85\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n85\\%=\\frac{85}{100}=0.85\n$$\n\nSo, to find $85\\%$ of $\\$85,$ we multiply $\\$85$ by $0.85:$\n\n$$\n0.85\\cdot $85=$72.25\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sale price of the chair is $\\$72.25.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d2b1036-0ea4-5fd1-bfed-885dcbae618b" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function by Constructing a Revenue and Cost Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "435b2373-d036-5a72-a5b3-9b22809d8383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$50$ per night" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510933} {:answer/id #uuid "67c15ab1-b48d-50a4-92c6-9e51aa87b1fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$84$ per night" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511002} {:answer/id #uuid "a6a572cf-f451-578e-b062-948286b9f2a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$98$ per night" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511133} {:answer/id #uuid "d26f525d-acc9-5080-9da8-6d1c7218b145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$120$ per night" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511216} {:answer/id #uuid "e464d60b-ec47-553e-aef7-bedba5f73485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$106$ per night" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6a572cf-f451-578e-b062-948286b9f2a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$98$ per night" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511133} :answer-field/id #uuid "97e14268-ee5f-5656-86a1-d067419325eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2888ed58-7e1b-57a5-91ea-2124b7fe040e" :question/math-academy-id "q-18671" :question/problem "A hotel has $71$ rooms. The manager has observed that when the room rate is $\\$50$ per night, all rooms are occupied and, for every $\\$2$ increase in the rate, a single room is vacated. If the maintenance per rented room is $\\$4$ per night, what rate per room should the manager set to obtain the maximum profit?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that if there are $x$ *vacant* rooms, the hotel has $(71-x)$ occupied rooms, and the rate per room is $(50+2x)$ dollars. So, the total revenue (in dollars) is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =\\underbrace{(71-x)}_{\\text{occupied rooms}}\\times \\,\\,\\,\\underbrace{(50+2x)}_{\\text{rate per room}} \\\\  & =3\\,550+142x-50x-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\,550+92x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, for having $(71-x)$ rooms rented out, the hotel has a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =4(71-x) \\\\  & =284-4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\ P(x) & =3\\,550+92x-2{x}^{2}-(284-4x) \\\\  & =3\\,266+96x-2{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 71.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<71$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=96-4x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 96-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =96 \\\\ x & =24\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=24$ lies in the interval $0<x<71.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=24$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-4<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=24$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=24$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that the hotel will have a maximum profit level when there are $x=24$ vacant rooms. \n\nWe know that the rate per room is $(50+2x)$ dollars. If there are $24$ vacant rooms, then the rate per room is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50+2(24) & =$98.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the manager should charge a room rate of $\\$98$ per night."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d2b1036-0ea4-5fd1-bfed-885dcbae618b" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function by Constructing a Revenue and Cost Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e2d9015-7c6e-5b67-9a9c-095a3636dc1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$6.7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510981} {:answer/id #uuid "5fcc83c3-0427-5087-ba48-9b12c96afdf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1.65$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510984} {:answer/id #uuid "795b1cfc-a40e-577c-9727-d8a557ce295a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$6.3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511031} {:answer/id #uuid "c7738231-00cd-5bfb-9cbd-70ba13d1a6eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1.85$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511196} {:answer/id #uuid "fb06f779-6b7c-57da-960c-d293832340e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e2d9015-7c6e-5b67-9a9c-095a3636dc1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$6.7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510981} :answer-field/id #uuid "15bd47fc-61d9-56ee-8807-cdce49006fe0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8babed00-8ea0-55fe-9919-358ba07b0746" :question/math-academy-id "q-119098" :question/problem "A coffee shop can produce up to $500$ cups of coffee per day. When the price per cup of coffee is $\\$3,$ the coffee shop sells all of them, and, for each dollar increase in the price, $50$ cups remain unsold. If the cost for producing a cup of coffee is $\\$0.40,$ what price per cup should be set to obtain the maximum profit?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that if there are $x$ hundreds of *unsold* cups of coffee, the coffee shop sells $(5-x)$ hundreds of cups of coffee, and the price per cup is $(3+2x)$ dollars. So, the total revenue (in hundreds of dollars) is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =\\underbrace{(5-x)}_{\\text{sold cups of coffee}}\\times \\,\\,\\,\\underbrace{(3+2x)}_{\\text{price per cup}} \\\\  & =15+10x-3x-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =15+7x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, for selling $(5-x)$ hundreds of cups of coffee, the coffee shop has a cost (in hundreds of dollars) of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =0.4(5-x) \\\\  & =2-0.4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\ P(x) & =(15+7x-2{x}^{2})-(2-0.4x) \\\\  & =13+7.4x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 5.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<5.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=7.4-4x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 7.4-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =7.4 \\\\ x & =1.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=1.85$ lies in the interval $0<x<5.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=1.85$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-4<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=1.85$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=1.85$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that the coffee shop will have a maximum profit level when there are $185$ unsold cups of coffee.  \n\nWe know that the price per cup is $(3+2x)$ dollars. If there are $1.85$ hundreds of unsold cups of coffee, then the price per cup is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+2(1.85) & =$6.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coffee shop should sell each cup of coffee for $\\$6.7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba5bf1d9-f6e2-5494-91fd-31c20d7fa09b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Rectangular Solid: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05308cdc-72ad-53cb-8842-72a8ffd93c02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$400$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512116} {:answer/id #uuid "2422f0ee-d558-5812-be60-ff74a4a72d4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$900$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512179} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc997cf-68a9-5c5a-a91f-44f31f08abc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$800$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512207} {:answer/id #uuid "d7435a38-2651-52af-beed-87c2b38bfbff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$500$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512611} {:answer/id #uuid "fc5e551c-a4be-5e65-a87d-376cd2db9ece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$450$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2422f0ee-d558-5812-be60-ff74a4a72d4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$900$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512179} :answer-field/id #uuid "45a670a5-6eca-551c-9bba-97922b332700" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "751e0aa2-a931-559b-9a2f-5250360d1346" :question/math-academy-id "q-118876" :question/problem "A company rents ten identical storage units in the form of a rectangular prism. Each unit is $5\\,\\text{yd}$ long, $3\\,\\text{yd}$ wide, and $3\\text{yd}$ high. If it costs $\\$2$ per cubic yard per day to rent a storage unit, how much does it cost the company each day to rent the storage units?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the volume of one storage unit.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=5,\\,w=3,\\,h=3\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=5\\cdot 3\\cdot 3=45\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe total volume of the ten units is\n\n$$\n45\\cdot 10=450\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to rent the storage units, each day the company must pay\n\n$$\n450\\cdot $2=$900.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d550bb4f-72a6-5b74-b050-8fdc2af6b23f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Total Price from a Unit Price"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10245252-29bb-5324-a581-26085a39fe28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$4.94$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513572} {:answer/id #uuid "16a66103-9d90-5322-91a9-b6156d9743b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$5.84$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513590} {:answer/id #uuid "258a35a6-2b3a-5e86-8b1f-0ce644f7d05d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$5.64$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513638} {:answer/id #uuid "afb02c14-0e39-54c3-8fb8-8b2b3a885193" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$5.34$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513994} {:answer/id #uuid "fd6a0e9a-46c1-5683-94a3-cd1426ac3273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$5.94$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afb02c14-0e39-54c3-8fb8-8b2b3a885193" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$5.34$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513994} :answer-field/id #uuid "62ea6bc2-70ac-5760-9ddc-ebf3b618c556" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c993b31b-1739-52ac-a2f5-55deea55eaf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-68863" :question/problem "Jack bought a pumpkin that weighed $3$ kilograms. The retail price of pumpkin at the store is  $\\$1.78/kg.$ How much did  Jack pay for the pumpkin?" :question/worked-solution "The unit price of pumpkin is $\\$1.78/kg.$ To find the cost of  $3$ kilograms of pumpkin, we multiply:    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.78\\times 3 & =5.34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Jack paid  $\\$5.34$ for the $3$-$\\text{kg}$ pumpkin."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60ccce15-6e30-58e5-a087-52100ebb460b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Unit Price With Conversion to Smaller Units"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18dfcd21-294b-55e4-b41f-375155c5f78f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2.30$ per cup" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517533} {:answer/id #uuid "270cd63f-c410-53f7-a265-b36e01146a3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2.20$ per cup" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517560} {:answer/id #uuid "478695fa-75cc-5df3-922d-ff3817bd57ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2.15$ per cup" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517626} {:answer/id #uuid "78971372-655e-56bd-ab00-f993d8f89951" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2.25$ per cup" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517715} {:answer/id #uuid "87ba484a-f3aa-5bb9-95e7-40634c5f2b3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2.10$ per cup" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78971372-655e-56bd-ab00-f993d8f89951" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2.25$ per cup" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517715} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee67847b-4ddb-5e29-b2ae-8486ce2de2d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "533e8f29-fadf-5200-8bf2-91c4cb3d254a" :question/math-academy-id "q-354907" :question/problem "Purchasing homemade lemonade costs $\\$36$ per gallon. What's the unit price per cup?\n\nAssume that there are $16$ cups in one gallon." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that there are $16$ cups in $1$ gallon. So, to find the unit price per cup, we divide the total price by $16:$\n\n$$\n36÷16=2.25\n$$\n\nTherefore, purchasing the lemonade costs $\\$2.25$ per cup."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60ccce15-6e30-58e5-a087-52100ebb460b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Unit Price With Conversion to Smaller Units"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d715d96-7916-5406-9aba-de7f90a6a3bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$0.16$ per ounce" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517699} {:answer/id #uuid "907d0e63-aa0e-53bd-ad9a-b19ed25389a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$0.15$ per ounce" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517759} {:answer/id #uuid "c0451957-dac5-5dee-8df2-a6987aa7df86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$0.18$ per ounce" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517856} {:answer/id #uuid "ed1a0d7c-ec15-522b-ac53-db82ebbd0e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$0.14$ per ounce" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517938} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5451f8-2cb8-55fb-8a4c-45725471904d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$0.12$ per ounce" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed1a0d7c-ec15-522b-ac53-db82ebbd0e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$0.14$ per ounce" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517938} :answer-field/id #uuid "311b09e1-10eb-57ba-a434-9673ef28e54e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "694684ae-3bf6-514c-9bb8-aef47c5c19c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-68552" :question/problem "At a convenience store, a $5$-pound canister of licorice candy costs $\\$11.20.$ What's the unit price per ounce?\n\nAssume that there are $16$ ounces in one pound." :question/worked-solution "First, we convert from pounds to ounces. There are $16$ ounces in each pound, so we multiply the number of pounds  by $16:$  \n\n$$\n5\\times 16=80\n$$\n\nSo, $5$ pounds is the same as $80$ ounces. Now, to find the unit price per ounce, we divide the total price by $80:$  \n\n$$\n11.20÷80=0.14\n$$\n\nTherefore, the licorice candy costs $\\$0.14$ per ounce."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "247d98e4-8f13-5536-8ad6-00a14e476caf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Unit Price With Conversion to Larger Units"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cc156c5-da42-5cf1-afb4-e1a9ccffe353" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$14$ per pound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517608} {:answer/id #uuid "89a61866-108b-5d5c-a9c6-f994eea7b3b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$10$ per pound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517745} {:answer/id #uuid "b2b7ba53-17b8-5b02-a5ce-b70700a2a8f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$16$ per pound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517824} {:answer/id #uuid "b3bd2153-3094-504a-9e6a-b74cf2b19e82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$20$ per pound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517827} {:answer/id #uuid "e3429c35-cffd-5c1d-a927-f2b3fb6ffd84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$12$ per pound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3429c35-cffd-5c1d-a927-f2b3fb6ffd84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$12$ per pound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517915} :answer-field/id #uuid "14b9646b-84f3-5527-a013-f8974a97792e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "924bf994-7977-5b78-af84-c2d13087032d" :question/math-academy-id "q-354914" :question/problem "Sarah bought $1$ ounce of almonds and paid $\\$0.75$ for it. What's the unit price per pound?\n\nAssume that there are $16$ ounces in one pound." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that there are $16$ ounces in each pound.  \n\nSince the price is $\\$0.75$ per ounce, to find the price per pound, we multiply by $16:$\n\n$$\n0.75\\times 16=12\n$$\n\nTherefore, the almonds cost $\\$12$ per pound."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60ccce15-6e30-58e5-a087-52100ebb460b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Unit Price With Conversion to Smaller Units"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "239c18ff-9dd1-57f6-8621-96f43e3689ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$3.65$ per quart" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517555} {:answer/id #uuid "580d3b9f-2181-5383-83ed-ba560ab18a67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$3.50$ per quart" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517651} {:answer/id #uuid "985dd1f2-b29f-5b02-881b-bb751aa3d19b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$3.60$ per quart" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517778} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e94df4-b05d-549d-b591-d4d9120e8455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$3.70$ per quart" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517885} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b9e68e-25ae-5105-8196-f8e7b86fd57c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$3.55$ per quart" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "985dd1f2-b29f-5b02-881b-bb751aa3d19b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$3.60$ per quart" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517778} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f5d24c7-c2ff-5561-a437-9575608cc9c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b6d906ea-b787-55f0-a205-6b6f9a337939" :question/math-academy-id "q-354900" :question/problem "Buying organic milk costs $\\$14.40$ per gallon. What's the unit price per quart?\n\nAssume that there are $4$ quarts in one gallon." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that there are $4$ quarts in $1$ gallon. So, to find the unit price per quart, we divide the total price by $4:$\n\n$$\n14.40÷4=3.60\n$$\n\nTherefore, the milk costs $\\$3.60$ per quart."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "247d98e4-8f13-5536-8ad6-00a14e476caf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Unit Price With Conversion to Larger Units"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "488a6733-2b62-5110-a8a6-e3949dc8cd8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$0.09$ per gallon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517629} {:answer/id #uuid "4de4fb3c-cdfc-5a86-ac04-dc62c62bcef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$16$ per gallon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517640} {:answer/id #uuid "4f9e2cfb-824b-5a25-96b9-7b7594c7e4ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$24$ per gallon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517644} {:answer/id #uuid "65a07d60-9deb-5380-9182-5f188866a085" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$0.90$ per gallon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517675} {:answer/id #uuid "c24e2006-b55b-5eed-b5f2-8ce6d5a28b8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$28$ per gallon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f9e2cfb-824b-5a25-96b9-7b7594c7e4ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$24$ per gallon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517644} :answer-field/id #uuid "be180afd-ef20-5827-b2f6-fd91c64375fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1dac973-b461-5ce9-a7ae-b3034ec42db1" :question/math-academy-id "q-354645" :question/problem "Maria bought $1$ cup of olive oil and paid $\\$1.50$ for it. What's the unit price per gallon?\n\nAssume that there are $16$ cups in one gallon." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that there are $16$ cups in each gallon.  \n\nSince the price is $\\$1.50$ per cup, to find the price per gallon, we multiply by $16:$\n\n$$\n1.50\\times 16=24\n$$\n\nTherefore, the olive oil costs $\\$24$ per gallon."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536871 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c37cc3b9-6255-5fb6-bb61-6cae10cd9531" :knowledge-point/title "Adding a Positive Number to a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "489e5d74-a352-5e41-872e-d97d1152340f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-$23.10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536395} {:answer/id #uuid "4ab57578-c0f4-564e-9dcc-b30c65737168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-$17.50$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536401} {:answer/id #uuid "4b45565c-a329-5dbb-9963-42bd3aefa10e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-$18.60$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536403} {:answer/id #uuid "c55f0115-e6bf-5e7b-88ff-bcccaff67b54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-$20.90$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536597} {:answer/id #uuid "db7af467-35db-5c13-913d-0211eaf4b4c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-$21.40$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db7af467-35db-5c13-913d-0211eaf4b4c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-$21.40$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536626} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc9bd1b9-0131-55b4-a33d-f2d8e3052b46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac3e396f-e9ff-515e-95c3-ddf87162b016" :question/math-academy-id "q-68844" :question/problem "Maria has a bank account at LJB Bank. She has an overdraft facility authorized by this bank. Her bank balance on Monday was $-\\$40.00.$ To reduce her debt, Maria decides to make a deposit of $\\$18.60$ into her bank account on the following Tuesday. What is Maria's balance at the end of the day on Tuesday?" :question/worked-solution "To determine the balance at the end of the day on Tuesday, we must add the amount of money Maria deposited to the starting balance. This gives\n\n$$\n-$40.00+$18.60=-$21.40.\n$$\n\nThus, Maria's balance at the end of the day on Tuesday is $-\\$21.40.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c30919c-c2b9-5e09-8512-98fcbe339564" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function Given a Revenue and Cost Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "141d1a25-fba8-5358-bdf7-47d4083d0230" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$243\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537267} {:answer/id #uuid "4e64df2e-399d-5b8f-a2f3-3cf2f8c50241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$357\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537391} {:answer/id #uuid "6758ee03-03a0-5b60-bde7-55ba18790097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$270\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537432} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3fc12f-fcff-5a8f-956b-4326f0c686ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$292\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537440} {:answer/id #uuid "9619c210-60c8-533d-bf4f-57ac601c6744" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$412\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6758ee03-03a0-5b60-bde7-55ba18790097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$270\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537432} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbe295f2-3c78-53d7-a4bc-8cad7afb171c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4cb999cb-e9bf-5cd0-a21c-a3857e783014" :question/math-academy-id "q-87593" :question/problem "A factory spends $C(x)=100-47x+21{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ thousand dollars per month to manufacture $x$ units of a certain product. The factory sells the product for $\\$100\\,000$ per unit. Given that the factory can produce between $1$ and $10$ items per month, what is the maximum monthly profit it can make?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product per month, the factory has a monthly revenue (income) of $R(x)=100\\cdot x=100x$ thousand dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function (in thousands of dollars) is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =100x-(100-47x+21{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-21{x}^{2}+147x-100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $1\\le x\\le 10.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=10$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $1<x<10.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-42x+147,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-42x+147 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-14x+49 & =0 \\\\ (x-7)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check if $x=7$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=6x-42.\n$$\n\nEvaluating ${P}^{″}(x)$ at the critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(7)=6(7)-42=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich does not confirm or deny that $x=7$ is a maximum of $P(x).$ Therefore, we should consider $x=7$ as one of the candidates.\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,$ $x=7$ and $x=10.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =(1)^{3}-21(1)^{2}+147(1)-100=27 \\\\ P(7) & =(7)^{3}-21(7)^{2}+147(7)-100=243 \\\\ P(10) & =(10)^{3}-21(10)^{2}+147(10)-100=270\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum monthly profit is $270\\cdot \\$1\\,000=\\$270\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba5bf1d9-f6e2-5494-91fd-31c20d7fa09b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Rectangular Solid: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1925be10-596c-557e-b94e-7843f3e27ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$47.72$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539530} {:answer/id #uuid "2c6bbdb9-99c3-511d-98b5-292b6419acef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$49.95$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539562} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a0135e-96f1-5953-b50e-81d6419a564b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$57.42$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539781} {:answer/id #uuid "c0a2dbc0-3274-5cf1-8bab-4898a8a2fd18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$54.88$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539812} {:answer/id #uuid "f17db65a-f656-5936-ad46-cb4b659e7dad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$50.25$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0a2dbc0-3274-5cf1-8bab-4898a8a2fd18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$54.88$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539812} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab997a9e-66ed-5632-8075-dc62b6beef15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "014f97dd-12f1-54e9-8347-3c53a77026ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-107731" :question/problem "![](images/62/6240abf268497f82475003619689d979b0b00678b4d49972ff2fbcf1fa866a9c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a cubic gas tank. One-fifth of the volume of the tank is filled with gas. If the fuel costs $\\$0.0016$ per cubic centimeter, how much would it cost to fill the tank to capacity?" :question/worked-solution "The length of $7\\,\\text{cm}$ shown in the diagram represents one-fifth of the tank's side length. Therefore, the length of each side of the tank is\n\n$$\n5\\cdot 7=35\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nThe empty space in the cube is a rectangular prism that is $35\\,\\text{cm}$ long, $35\\,\\text{cm}$ wide, and $35-7=28\\,\\text{cm}$ high.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=35\\,\\text{cm},\\,w=35\\,\\text{cm},\\,h=28\\,\\text{cm}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV=35\\cdot 35\\cdot 28=34\\,300\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to fill the tank to capacity, it would cost\n\n$$\n34\\,300\\cdot 0.0016=$54.88.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f90ea994-79f2-5409-bf96-306803b4ef01" :knowledge-point/title "Applying a Percentage Increase: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ca874c1-4215-5fa3-9f9b-fc5a7fcfea4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$70.25$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541479} {:answer/id #uuid "24b8c98f-8ea6-5177-8cce-121d4ea2ad13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$71.00$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541498} {:answer/id #uuid "52d1a82b-37fe-5eb7-b559-3be5ae2a38d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$68.96$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541591} {:answer/id #uuid "be402325-f576-53ec-ac7a-edffe8a73154" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$69.50$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541821} {:answer/id #uuid "e7964137-d215-566b-8387-07ced5b59223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$66.35$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52d1a82b-37fe-5eb7-b559-3be5ae2a38d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$68.96$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541591} :answer-field/id #uuid "471f8877-1cd7-5a08-8ddd-83f1e22878b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16908ce6-e741-598e-9537-76866b0601d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-326520" :question/problem "A backpack costs $\\$64$ before tax. If a $7.75\\%$ sales tax is applied, what is the total price after tax?" :question/worked-solution "To find the total price after tax, we apply a $7.75\\%$ increase to the original price.\n\nIncreasing by $7.75\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%+7.75\\%=107.75\\%=1.0775\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original price by $1.0775:$\n\n$$\n64\\times 1.0775=68.96.\n$$\n\nSo, the total price of the backpack after tax is $\\$68.96.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554645 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e77a14c-9943-5494-ae89-a77c7a1888d0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Residuals From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d738011-9f4c-5d4e-a309-ef64a768c823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1\\,200$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554051} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b7e43c-40c5-5e6a-932e-71b4efd2aa35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554247} {:answer/id #uuid "d48ea0cd-c010-5dea-b3a7-cfb311eab498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554330} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b52400-e104-580d-9769-33accdba452b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1\\,500$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554362} {:answer/id #uuid "eb398d66-b3c7-5535-b999-ccfa4a862a14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2\\,500$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d48ea0cd-c010-5dea-b3a7-cfb311eab498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554330} :answer-field/id #uuid "2802197e-59f8-5a42-85de-d389505bc4f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd84baa2-dbd3-5523-aca7-04ab311e0400" :question/math-academy-id "q-134797" :question/problem "![](images/81/815ba0a13bc8cbd7adcfadc63b80d6f2da36c9755166694915fa2f297be27721.png)\n\nAn employee of a car dealership collects data on the value $y$ (in thousands of dollars) of a particular car model $x$ years after its release to the market. The scatter plot for the data and the corresponding linear regression line are shown above.\n\nAccording to the regression model, what is the residual for the highlighted datapoint?" :question/worked-solution "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe highlighted datapoint has coordinates $(6,12).$ So, our actual value is $y=12.$\n\nOn the other hand, the point on the regression line that corresponds to $x=6$ has the $y$-coordinate $10.$ So, our estimated value is $\\widehat{y}=10.$\n\n![](images/ab/ab5e194ac03482b1cee6bdb7aa35e323b40a1ad63769e60acbb000adb7aa1e82.png)\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\widehat{y} \\\\  & =12-10 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residual is $\\$2\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555868 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcd3e634-d64c-57dc-9d4c-8f7f67c731ab" :knowledge-point/title "Creating an Exponential Growth Expression Using Percentage Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25bfa7fe-6bbc-5bba-8456-d285f71fb72d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$13\\,430$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555077} {:answer/id #uuid "3aec392f-ba73-525c-8b12-dfcceda07e38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$15\\,660$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555127} {:answer/id #uuid "4a58dba6-5ed5-5633-95b8-7e88afe39c63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$14\\,050$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555161} {:answer/id #uuid "bd58d7b9-4b69-5345-98e8-c2b4988aeceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$15\\,970$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555450} {:answer/id #uuid "ce20cd5b-5a77-5b24-a7f2-4481ecd81f4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$14\\,830$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a58dba6-5ed5-5633-95b8-7e88afe39c63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$14\\,050$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555161} :answer-field/id #uuid "04d9141d-29ea-5c02-a238-48a843d162a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ebeb4e55-43ec-53a6-b8e8-b7cfc99f6520" :question/math-academy-id "q-61650" :question/problem "Rose's initial salary was $\\$10\\,000$, and her salary increased by $12\\%$ per year. To the nearest $\\$10$, what was Rose's salary $3$ years later?" :question/worked-solution "Rose received $\\$10\\,000$ initially, and this amount increased by $12\\%$ each year.\n\n$12\\%$ as a decimal is $0.12.$ Therefore, an increase of $12\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.12=1.12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ year, Rose received $10\\,000\\cdot (1.12)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, Rose received $10\\,000\\cdot (1.12)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, Rose received $10\\,000\\cdot (1.12)^{3}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the expression for the amount of money received after $3$ years, we get\n\n$$\n10\\,000\\cdot (1.12)^{3}≈14\\,050.\n$$\n\nAfter $3$ years, Rose received approximately $14\\,050$ dollars."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01f99115-f376-50c2-9b7e-79800006d437" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Value Given a Ratio by Rounding an Amount"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3753fd0b-c081-5341-ae6b-6434522711ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$900$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557978} {:answer/id #uuid "45d235ac-7712-5ca4-877d-63407f23d444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$600$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558006} {:answer/id #uuid "5b98ffd4-63ed-5add-bf98-ecf5d6492c8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$760$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558042} {:answer/id #uuid "807be663-7835-5fd3-82ec-350b159ded0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$700$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558117} {:answer/id #uuid "881d6191-65af-5d92-988e-e00ce240a7fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$840$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45d235ac-7712-5ca4-877d-63407f23d444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$600$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558006} :answer-field/id #uuid "0db908f9-7a3a-5a7e-a3ec-6a328a11d897" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "92ab7ff7-d02e-5687-bfb0-2e6e2beec2cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-364163" :question/problem "Maya and Leon get paid for painting houses. The ratio of Maya's reward to Leon's reward is $3:4.$ \nDuring a particular week, Leon received $\\$806.$ By rounding Leon's earnings to the nearest hundred, approximate the amount of money that Maya received." :question/worked-solution "First, let's approximate the amount of money that Leon received:\n\n$$\n$806⟶$800\n$$\n\nThe ratio of Maya's earnings to Leon's earnings is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe numerator represents Maya's earnings  and the denominator represents Leon's earnings.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $800$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by \n $200$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 200}{4\\times 200}=\\frac{600}{800}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Maya received approximately $\\$600.$"}]}
